Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Relation to Hill\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Model", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.530448147058743*^9, 
  3.530448156562306*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a01abadf-634e-4d8e-a839-\
c22bf130960d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["a", "1"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "543a52ff-82c9-44aa-8a3e-fe53a66560b4"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["a", "2"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8c8f8f8c-6e10-451e-8855-f51dd74f80f4"],
 ", etc. are close to 1\[NonBreakingSpace](i.e.: if the cooperativity is \
strong), then the velocity will be dominated by the term with the highest \
power of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["K", "S"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3a99f239-3564-4cac-9e6c-2d2be6dc0751"],
 ". This is equivalent to Hill's interpretation that the binding of all \
substrate ligands practically occurs simultaneously, and all terms with \
exponents lower than ",
 StyleBox["n",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " can be neglected. According to Hill, the following dependency describes \
cooperativity to a sufficient degree:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGAQAWIQ/SE37/1x71eON8LOfAbRK9Qr+o74vHIs+mA/GUS3
XdkS/xZIR62ekQaiTTn7kt8B6YB7vukgeh3/pvivQHpP+PkUEK2WIrjhB5C+
